LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Item master through a buyer's eyes", "15", "<p>Selling 2 walked the item master for sellers; this chapter walks the SAME record for buyers, because one item record serves both sides of trade and the buy-side fields decide whether procurement runs on data or on memory. The frame to carry: the item is one identity with two faces — what we charge lives on the selling face; what it costs us, who supplies it, how long it takes to arrive, and when to reorder live on the face this module owns.</p><ul><li><b>Is Purchase Item</b> — the buy-side gate, the exact mirror of Is Sales Item: ticked, the item can appear on material requests, purchase orders, receipts and purchase invoices; unticked, it is invisible to procurement no matter what stock exists. Items can be both (normal trading stock), purchase-only (consumables, spares, services you buy but never sell), or sales-only (items you make, not buy). The first diagnostic for the-item-is-missing-from-my-PO is this checkbox — and note the symptom distinction that transfers from selling: a gated-out item does not APPEAR; an unpriced item appears at rate 0.</li><li><b>Default Purchase UOM</b> — the unit you BUY in, which is routinely not the unit you count in (Chapter 2's whole subject).</li><li><b>Lead Time Days</b> — how long this item takes from order to arrival. Not decoration: planning arithmetic (Chapter 5) reads it, and an honest lead time on imports (weeks, not days) is the difference between reorder points that work and stockouts with paperwork.</li><li><b>Safety Stock, Reorder Level, Reorder Qty</b> — the replenishment triggers, per warehouse where it matters (Chapter 5).</li><li><b>Last Purchase Rate</b> — maintained by the system from actual purchase documents: what you LAST paid. A rear-view mirror, invaluable for trend reading (Chapter 6) and dangerous when mistaken for an agreed price (Chapter 6 convicts that habit).</li><li><b>Valuation fields</b> — the method by which stock carries cost (Chapter 4's literacy), and the account defaults your accountant confirmed.</li><li><b>Serial/batch flags</b> — decided at creation (Selling 2 taught why); the buy-side consequence is that RECEIVING demands the identities (Buying 6), so a pharma item's batch flag is a procurement obligation before it is a selling one.</li></ul><p><b>The one-record principle.</b> Resist the urge to create purchase-side twins of selling items (PARA-500-BUY next to PARA-500): one physical product is ONE item record wearing both flags, or the stock ledger splits into two half-truths that no report can join. The duplicate disease's item-master edition — third master, same law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Purchase UOMs & the conversion arithmetic", "14", "<p>Nigerian procurement buys in cartons, bales, drums, and pallets — and counts stock in pieces. Selling 2 built the UOM machinery outbound; this chapter runs it INBOUND, where getting it wrong doesn't misprice a quotation — it miscounts the warehouse on arrival day.</p><p><b>The three-unit picture, buy-side.</b> The item's <b>Default UOM</b> remains the stock-keeping unit — what bins and valuations speak (Units, if the warehouse counts pieces; that decision was made once and governs both faces). The <b>Default Purchase UOM</b> is the trade unit purchases default to — Carton-12 for the paracetamol you buy by the dozen-pack. Each purchase document row then carries its UOM and the <b>conversion factor</b> to the stock unit: a PO row of 50 Carton-12 is, and displays as, 600 Units to the stock ledger; the receipt that follows moves 600 Units into the bin while the paperwork talks cartons to the supplier. Same arithmetic as selling, opposite direction of travel — and the same law: <b>every rate and every quantity knows its unit</b>.</p><p><b>Rates ride the UOM.</b> The supplier's price is per THEIR unit: ₦9,600 per Carton-12, not ₦800 typed against a unit row. The classic inbound bug is the mirror of selling's: a per-carton rate on a per-unit row (a PO for 600 priced as if 600 cartons — the approval that should have been ₦480k reading ₦5.76m, or the reverse, an underpriced order the supplier corrects with an invoice surprise). The prevention is the discipline already built: rate, UOM, and conversion factor read together, always.</p><p><b>Receiving is where UOM errors become physical.</b> The order said 50 cartons; the truck holds 50 cartons; the receiving clerk enters… what? If the receipt row is in Cartons with factor 12, entering 50 is correct and 600 Units land in the bin. If someone re-keys it as 600 in a Carton row — or 50 in a Unit row — the bin is wrong by a factor of twelve, and it is wrong at the exact moment the goods physically arrived, which is the hardest moment to argue with later. Buying 6 owns receiving craft; the UOM foundation is laid here: <i>the receipt speaks the PO's units, and the conversion does the counting</i>.</p><p><b>Split-carton reality.</b> Suppliers short-ship: 47 cartons and a loose half-dozen. The honest row drops to the unit that matches physical reality (Selling 5's carton-corner rule, inbound edition): 47 Carton-12 plus 6 Units, two rows, both converting truthfully — never 47.5 cartons the warehouse cannot count.</p><p><b>Purchase UOM vs supplier pack changes.</b> When a manufacturer moves from 12-packs to 10-packs, that is a NEW UOM (Carton-10) with its own factor — used from the change date forward — not an edit to Carton-12's factor, which would silently rewrite the meaning of every historical document that used it. Conversion factors are history's vocabulary; you add words, you do not redefine them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Buying price lists & supplier rates", "14", "<p>Selling 2 taught price lists as the architecture of who pays what; the buying side holds the mirror: <b>who charges you what</b>, encoded so documents fetch agreed rates instead of remembered ones.</p><p><b>The mechanics, mirrored.</b> A price list flagged <b>Buying</b> holds purchase rates; the Selling/Buying checkboxes on the list say which side it feeds, and a consultant never confuses them — the checkbox says. Rates live as <b>Item Price</b> atoms exactly as before: one item + one buying list + one rate, in the list's single currency, optionally validity-dated — and, the buy-side power, <b>scopable to a supplier</b>: the same item carrying ₦9,600/carton from Emzor and ₦9,850/carton from the backup distributor as two supplier-scoped records, each fetched when its supplier's document asks. Agreed rates per supplier are thereby STRUCTURAL — the negotiation's output stored where every PO reads it.</p><p><b>The defaulting chain, inbound.</b> The purchase document's price list lands from the supplier's default buying list (Buying 1, Chapter 4's chain), else the global buying default in Buying Settings; each row's rate looks up item + list (+ supplier scope, + UOM coherence, + validity date). Recite it cold, because the rate-0 clinic transfers verbatim: <i>a purchase row at 0.00 means the list asked had no row for the item</i> — not an error, an honest empty answer — and the diagnostic walk is the same four checks (right list? record exists? right UOM's rate? validity window covers today?).</p><p><b>Agreed rate vs invoice rate.</b> The buying list holds what was AGREED; the purchase invoice will eventually say what was CHARGED. Buying 7's three-way match is where disagreement surfaces formally, but the master-data posture starts here: when a supplier's new price is accepted, the Item Price is updated through the owned workflow — so drift between the list and reality is a signal (the supplier moved and nobody encoded it; or the invoice is wrong and the list is your evidence), never just noise. A firm whose buying lists are current negotiates from records; a firm whose lists fossilised negotiates from the supplier's memory.</p><p><b>Bulk maintenance, mirrored.</b> Quarter-start cost moves hit hundreds of purchase rates; the workflow is the same export → edit → re-import in update mode, with the same ownership discipline — one door, one owner, one trail. FX-driven repricing of import-side lists (the USD list's naira consequences) is Chapter 7's thread.</p><p><b>What buying lists are NOT.</b> Not a cost-accounting system (valuation carries cost — Chapter 4), not a substitute for RFQ comparison on new sourcing (Buying 4 compares live quotes; the list stores the WINNER), and not per-PO scratch space — the rate on a document may be negotiated down in the moment exactly as selling's Rate vs Price-List-Rate pair allowed, visible and reportable, while the list stands as reference.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Cost, valuation & what a thing truly costs", "14", "<p>Three different numbers all get called <i>cost</i>, and a procurement professional keeps them distinct, because each answers a different question and confusing them misprices decisions.</p><p><b>Number one: the purchase rate.</b> What the supplier charges per unit — the buying list's agreed figure, the PO's negotiated figure. It is the PRICE of buying, not yet the cost of owning.</p><p><b>Number two: the landed cost.</b> What it took to get the unit onto your shelf: purchase rate PLUS freight, clearing, duty, insurance, handling. For local purchases the gap is small; for imports it is the whole story — a $10 unit at the factory can be a ₦25,000 unit on the Lagos shelf once the container's costs spread across its contents. Chapter 7 frames the thinking; Buying 6 carries the mechanics of adding these charges to received stock. The reflex to build now: <b>for imports, the invoice price is an ingredient, not the answer</b>.</p><p><b>Number three: the valuation rate.</b> What the stock ledger carries the unit at — the number that flows to cost-of-goods when Selling's documents ship it, and the number margin reports subtract. It derives from actual receipts (at their landed costs) under the company's <b>valuation method</b>: the working literacy is one paragraph — <i>FIFO</i> consumes cost in arrival order (old stock's cost leaves first), <i>Moving Average</i> blends each receipt into a running average — the method is an accounting-policy choice made with the accountant, applied consistently, and a consultant's job is knowing which is in force and what it implies for how margins move when purchase prices move, not re-litigating it per item.</p><p><b>Why this hygiene is a SELLING feature too.</b> Selling 8's below-cost guard (Validate Selling Price Against Purchase Rate) is only as good as the purchase-side truth beneath it: garbage purchase rates make the floor a random number generator — the exact caveat that module stated, whose fix lives HERE. And your team's remediation history is the cautionary tale at scale: opening stock keyed at selling prices, receipts at guessed rates, and valuations drifting from reality produce the multi-warehouse corrections that consume months. Cost truth is cheap at entry and ruinous to reconstruct.</p><p><b>The professional's habit.</b> When anyone says <i>cost</i>, ask which one: the supplier's price (negotiate it), the landed figure (manage it), or the valuation (trust it, because the first two were recorded honestly). Reports that mix them — margin computed against purchase rate while valuation says otherwise, or import viability judged on factory price — are the analytical bugs this chapter exists to prevent.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Lead times, reorder points & the planning fields", "14", "<p>Procurement's deepest purpose is that the shelf never surprises anyone. The item master carries four planning fields that, filled honestly, let replenishment run as arithmetic — and, left blank, guarantee that buying stays a panic-driven art.</p><p><b>Lead Time Days — the honest number.</b> Order-to-arrival, for THIS item from its usual source: 3 days from the Lagos distributor, 45-60 from the Mumbai factory through clearing. The field wants the honest planning figure including the boring parts (approval time, the clearing queue), because everything downstream multiplies by it. The classic dishonesty is optimism — the 30-day lead time that is really 50 — which converts every reorder into a three-week stockout with perfect paperwork.</p><p><b>Reorder Level — the trigger.</b> The stock quantity at which replenishment must START for arrival before exhaustion. The arithmetic that sets it: expected consumption over the lead time, plus the safety margin. An item selling 20 units/day with a 10-day lead needs the trigger around 200 plus safety — pulled lower, you stock out during the wait; parked absurdly high, you warehouse cash. It can be set <b>per warehouse</b>, because the Ikeja store and the Ibadan store consume differently.</p><p><b>Reorder Qty — the amount.</b> How much to order when triggered: the batch that balances carton economics, supplier minimums, storage, and cash. Often the supplier's economic pack (whole pallets), sometimes the month's cover — a commercial choice the field records so the system can propose it.</p><p><b>Safety Stock — the buffer.</b> The floor held against variability: demand spikes, supplier slips, clearing delays. Bigger for erratic-demand and long-lead items; near zero for stable local lines. Safety stock is insurance, and like insurance it is priced — the premium is carrying cost, and the claim it pays is the sale not lost.</p><p><b>What the fields DO.</b> With levels set, the system can raise <b>Material Requests automatically</b> when projected stock crosses the trigger — the auto-replenishment machinery Buying 3 owns. The projected-quantity arithmetic you already know from Selling 4 (actual minus reserved plus incoming) is exactly what the trigger watches: incoming purchase orders count toward coverage, reserved sales count against it, so the whole trading position feeds one honest number per item per warehouse.</p><p><b>The maintenance rhythm.</b> Planning fields rot: consumption shifts seasonally, lead times move with suppliers and ports, last year's safety stock funds this year's overstock. The review is quarterly and exception-driven — the items that stocked out (trigger too low? lead time dishonest?) and the items that never turn (level too high? demand gone?) — fifteen minutes per category, read straight from stock reports. Fields set once in 2024 and never revisited are how firms simultaneously stock out of winners and warehouse losers.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Reading prices: last purchase rate & the trend discipline", "13", "<p>The system remembers what you actually paid; this chapter is the craft of reading that memory — and the conviction of its misuse.</p><p><b>Last Purchase Rate — the rear-view mirror.</b> Maintained automatically from purchase documents: the most recent actual rate for the item. Its honest uses: a sanity reference when raising ad-hoc purchases (does this quote resemble what we last paid?), a drift detector against the buying list (last-paid pulling away from agreed means the supplier moved and nobody encoded it — Chapter 3's signal), and the seed for trend reading below.</p><p><b>The conviction: last-paid is not agreed.</b> The habit to break in every client office: treating last purchase rate AS the price — raising POs at last-paid, accepting whatever the last invoice said as the new normal. That habit hands repricing power to whoever issued the last invoice: one padded bill and the padding becomes your reference, compounding invisibly. The agreed rate lives on the buying list, negotiated and encoded (Chapter 3); last-paid is EVIDENCE about the world, not POLICY for it — the same instrument-vs-record distinction as selling's Price List Rate vs Rate, wearing procurement clothes.</p><p><b>Trend reading, monthly.</b> Purchase-rate movement per item group per quarter is procurement's inflation instrument: which categories are drifting, which suppliers move first, where naira weakness lands hardest (the import-side groups from Buying 1's tree). Three reads matter: <i>category drift</i> (feeds selling's repricing rhythm — Selling 2's quarter-start import workflow is DOWNSTREAM of this read, which is how cost moves reach selling prices deliberately instead of through margin erosion); <i>supplier divergence</i> (two suppliers of the same item drifting apart is a renegotiation or a resourcing flag — Buying 4's RFQ machinery is the action); and <i>single-item spikes</i> (a step-change in one item's cost is either a pack-size change wearing a price costume — Chapter 2's UOM check first — or a real move worth a phone call).</p><p><b>The price file discipline.</b> Where negotiations conclude, the result is encoded the same day: the buying list updated through the owned workflow, validity dates carrying agreed windows (the six-month locked rate expiring on schedule), supplier scope keeping multi-source items honest. The firm whose buying lists are living documents reads every invoice against a reference; the firm whose lists fossilised reads every invoice against a feeling.</p><p><b>What this buys selling.</b> Close the loop consciously: honest purchase rates power the below-cost floor (Selling 8), honest valuation powers margin truth (Chapter 4), and the trend read times the selling-side repricing. Procurement's data discipline is half of selling's margin story — the half the sales team never sees and always depends on.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Import costing: thinking in landed terms", "13", "<p>For an import-dependent trading economy, the difference between a profitable line and a quietly loss-making one is usually not the factory price — it is everything that happens between the factory and the shelf. This chapter installs landed-cost THINKING; Buying 6 wires the mechanics.</p><p><b>The stack, itemised.</b> A container's true cost assembles from: the supplier's invoice (FOB or CIF — know which, because insurance and freight are inside CIF and outside FOB); ocean or air freight; marine insurance; import duty and levies at the tariff line; clearing and terminal charges; the agent's fees; haulage to your warehouse; and the FX rate at which each naira figure crystallised. A $10.00 FOB unit can land at ₦24,000–₦28,000 depending on duty band and the naira's mood — and a buyer negotiating cents off the factory price while the clearing chain leaks thousands per carton is optimising the visible tenth of the iceberg.</p><p><b>Spreading the stack.</b> Landed charges attach to RECEIVED STOCK, spread across the shipment's items (by value or quantity as appropriate), so each unit's valuation carries its true cost — the mechanics are Buying 6's landed-cost treatment at receiving. The thinking to install now: charges belong ON the stock they landed, not in a general expense bucket, because expensing them flatters gross margin (stock undervalued, COGS understated) while the P&amp;L's expense lines quietly eat the difference — a distortion that makes importing look better than it is until the year-end tells the truth.</p><p><b>FX at cost.</b> The USD invoice converts at the transaction's rate; the naira cost of a shipment is decided partly by WHEN obligations crystallise. The record-keeping discipline (rates on documents, dates honest) is what makes the question <i>what did this container really cost us in naira</i> answerable from records — and the exposure question <i>what happens to our costs at ₦50 weaker</i> answerable by arithmetic over the import-side groups instead of by dread.</p><p><b>Viability arithmetic, the habit.</b> Before committing to an import line: selling price ceiling (what the market bears — selling's data), landed cost floor (the full stack at honest FX), and the margin between them tested at pessimistic clearing times and rates. The routine kills two familiar failures: the line that was profitable at the old duty band, and the container ordered on factory-price maths that arrives underwater. One page per candidate line, revisited when any stack layer moves.</p><p><b>Documentation as cost control.</b> Buying 1 Chapter 7 set the filing habit — Form M, BL, PAAR, duty assessments against the purchase cycle. Here is its payoff: the stack is auditable per shipment, clearing-cost drift is visible across shipments (the agent whose fees creep; the terminal whose charges doubled), and the negotiation with every layer of the chain proceeds from records. Landed cost is not a number you accept; it is a stack you manage, layer by layer.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's purchase-side item architecture", "16", "<p>The same firm, the same items — now built from the buying chair. Selling 2 gave HealthTrade its selling architecture for PARA-500; this chapter completes the record's other face, and every step names its chapter.</p><p><b>Step 1 — the one record, both flags (Ch. 1).</b> PARA-500 already exists (Selling 2 built it); the buy-side face is completed on the SAME record: Is Purchase Item ✓ (no PARA-500-BUY twin — the one-record principle), Default Purchase UOM Carton-12 with its factor (the UOM set Selling 2 defined, now defaulting inbound documents), batch flag already on (the pharma receiving obligation noted for Buying 6).</p><p><b>Step 2 — supplier-scoped rates (Ch. 3).</b> The buying list holds three atoms for one item: Emzor at ₦9,600/Carton-12, the backup distributor at ₦9,850/Carton-12 (supplier-scoped — each PO fetches its supplier's agreed rate), and the Mumbai exporter's USD list carrying $6.20/Carton-12 for the import lane. Validity dates carry Emzor's six-month locked window; the expiry will force the renewal conversation on schedule.</p><p><b>Step 3 — planning fields, per reality (Ch. 5).</b> Consumption runs ~40 cartons/day across channels. Local lanes: lead time 3 days, reorder level set for the wait plus safety, reorder qty at Emzor's pallet economics. The import lane's fields tell the truth: 50 days lead through clearing, safety stock sized for port variability. The Ikeja and Ibadan warehouses carry their own levels — they consume differently, and the fields say so.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the landed file (Ch. 4, 7).</b> The import lane's viability page: $6.20 FOB, freight/insurance/duty/clearing stack at honest rates → landed ≈ ₦8,900/carton at current FX — undercutting Emzor by ₦700 IF the 50-day pipeline is funded and the naira holds; the page notes the break-even rate. The MD's import decision is now arithmetic with assumptions visible, not a feeling about factory prices.</p><p><b>Step 5 — the trend hooks (Ch. 6).</b> Last-purchase-rate drift alerts the buyer when Emzor's invoices pull away from ₦9,600 (encode or dispute — never silently absorb); the monthly category read feeds Selling 2's quarter-start repricing so cost moves reach selling prices by decision.</p><p><b>Step 6 — the receiving rehearsal (Ch. 2).</b> Before the first import arrives, the receiving team walks the UOM drill: the PO speaks Carton-12, the receipt enters cartons, the conversion counts units into the bin; the short-shipment case (47 cartons + 6 loose) is rehearsed as two honest rows. Buying 6 will own the craft; the vocabulary is already fluent.</p><p><b>What the afternoon bought:</b> one item, two faces, zero twins; agreed rates per supplier fetchable by every document; planning fields that replace panic with triggers; an import decision made in landed terms with its break-even visible; and price-trend hooks that keep both the buying list and the selling list honest. The item master is now the whole trade's single source of product truth — which is exactly what the law at the end of this module says.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & support patterns", "13", "<p>The purchase-item scar tissue: the tickets, the diseases, the fixes — and the module's law.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — \"The item will not appear on the purchase order.\"</b> Disease: Is Purchase Item unticked (Ch. 1) — the buy-side gate, first check always. The symptom distinction transfers: absent means gated out; present at 0.00 means unpriced (Pattern 2). Runner-up: the item is disabled entirely.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — \"Purchase rate shows 0.\"</b> The rate-0 clinic, inbound (Ch. 3): no Item Price on the document's buying list — or the record exists but under the wrong supplier scope, the wrong UOM, or an expired validity window. The four-check walk resolves it in one minute; the anti-fix (typing a remembered rate) is how lists fossilise.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — \"The bin is wrong by a factor of twelve since Tuesday's delivery.\"</b> The UOM receiving bug (Ch. 2): cartons keyed as units or units as cartons at the gate. Fix the stock through proper documents, then the drill — the receipt speaks the PO's units and the conversion counts. (At client scale this is the disease behind half the phantom-stock archaeology.)</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — \"Reorder never fires; we stock out with perfect paperwork.\"</b> Disease: planning fields blank or dishonest (Ch. 5) — no trigger exists, or the 30-day lead time is really 50. Fix: set levels from the consumption-times-lead arithmetic, tell the truth about lead times, and put the quarterly exception review in the calendar.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — \"Our margins look great until year-end.\"</b> Disease: landed charges expensed instead of attached to stock (Ch. 4, 7) — valuation understated, gross margin flattered, the P&amp;L eating the difference in expense lines. Fix: landed-cost treatment at receiving (Buying 6) and the viability pages that judge imports in landed terms.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — \"POs go out at whatever we last paid.\"</b> Disease: last-purchase-rate treated as the agreed price (Ch. 6) — repricing power handed to the last invoice. Fix: agreed rates encoded on the buying list through the owned workflow; last-paid demoted to evidence.</p><p><b>Pattern 7 — \"We have the same product twice — one for buying, one for selling.\"</b> Disease: the twin-record anti-pattern (Ch. 1) — stock split into half-truths, valuation and history unjoinable. Fix: merge to one record with both flags while the twin is young; prevention is the one-record principle taught at onboarding.</p><p><b>The law.</b> Buying 1 closed with: purchase documents are downstream of supplier masters. This module adds the second: <b>cost truth begins at the item</b> — the gate flags, the UOM arithmetic, the supplier-scoped agreed rates, the honest planning fields, and the landed-cost habit together decide whether every downstream number (the PO's value, the receipt's count, the valuation's margin, the reorder's timing) is computed or improvised. Selling's whole margin story stands on this module being done well — which is the quiet dignity of procurement data work: nobody celebrates it, and everything depends on it.</p>"),
]

